  "9896ed1d-dce9-4b73-9cdc-7da3d21f3b1d": {
   "id": "9896ed1d-dce9-4b73-9cdc-7da3d21f3b1d",
   "message": {
    "id": "9896ed1d-dce9-4b73-9cdc-7da3d21f3b1d",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1780540268.021,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "**Aggregation theory (Ben Thompson's framing):** on the internet, distribution costs fall to near zero, so power shifts from those who control supply to those who control *demand*, i.e., the user relationship. Aggregators (Google, Facebook, Netflix, Airbnb) offer a superior user experience, attract users, and that attracts suppliers, who then have to be on the aggregator on its terms. Key traits: a direct relationship with users, zero marginal cost to serve them, and demand-driven multi-sided networks that commoditize suppliers. The winner tends to get bigger because more users → more suppliers → better experience.\n\n**Where it doesn't cleanly hold:** markets where supply is scarce and differentiated, e.g. ride-hailing in some cities, where drivers multi-home across apps and users switch on price. Neither side is locked in, so the \"aggregator\" captures much less power than the theory predicts. Healthcare and enterprise software (with procurement and integration costs) are other weak fits."
     ]
    },
