  "1e3a71f6-2fe4-4313-b564-e48eeacf2b04": {
   "id": "1e3a71f6-2fe4-4313-b564-e48eeacf2b04",
   "message": {
    "id": "1e3a71f6-2fe4-4313-b564-e48eeacf2b04",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1780540418.803,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "Mostly no. An aggregator needs suppliers it can commoditize and near-zero cost to serve each new user on both sides. A research notebook is closer to a *tool*: its value comes from what it does with the user's own material, not from a supply side that has to be on the platform. Two partial exceptions:\n- If it became the place where researchers *discover* papers or collaborators, publishers and authors would be a supply side and aggregation dynamics could appear.\n- Integration ecosystems (chat apps, reference managers) could start competing for the notebook's users, but that's platform dynamics, not aggregation.\n\nSo the more useful frame for a notebook is switching costs and data gravity, not aggregation."
     ]
    },
